  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 852 PM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAn upper level shortwave ridge is currently parked west of \nSouthern New Mexico and West Texas. This has brought us clear and \ncalm conditions throughout the day. However, a positively tilted \nupper level trough will to dig further south. This will produce a \njet streak over western Colorado and combined with a lee surface \nlow will subsequently increase winds over the area. Saturday is \nforecast to be breezy and gust up to 30mph in the highlands. \nHowever, model and ensemble guidance are in agreement with the low\nstaying to our north; therefore, no wind advisories are \nanticipated at this time. Temperatures will also be above normal, \nwith highs in the low 70s for the lowlands. \n\nAs the trough attempts to push into our area, a high pressure will\npush the low eastward and out of area by Sunday early morning.\nThis will lead to calm winds and dry conditions.While an upper\nlevel high is parked over Far West Texas and Southern New Mexico,\na backdoor cold front will push through our area Sunday morning into\nMonday which should lower our temperatures back toward near \nseasonal while keeping winds speeds fairly light around 5 to 10 \nmph out of the Southeast. \n\nOnce the ridge moves through our area, a closed Pacific Low will\ntrek across the desert southwest and into our area by Thursday\nmorning. Model and NBM guidance are fairly confident that we\nshould see precipitation. However, confidence is lower on the\nexact precipitation type that will reach the surface, especially\nin the highlands. Currently, the Sacramento mountains are forecast\nto receive a wintry mix while the Gila and Black mountain ranges \ncould see snow and accumulations up to 6 inches. It will depend \non how cold the highlands get to determine how much snow, if any, \nthe Gila, Black, and Sacramento mountain ranges receive.",
